    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nReplaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), computed on creation.\n\n**Level rule** (`Owner.getMembershipLevel()`): start at 1; +1 when an email is present; +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0; capped at 3 (level 4 left reserved for tenure).\n\n### Files changed\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` (string) response property with `membershipLevel` (integer, 1\u20133). Since the `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec at build time, this swaps the field in the response.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Transient` derived `getMembershipLevel()` as the single source of truth for the rule. It's derived from the already-persisted `email` and `namesakeCount`, mirroring how the old tier was derived from persisted fields (so `GET` recomputes it consistently).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Removed the `membershipTier` MapStruct expression; MapStruct now auto-maps `membershipLevel` from the new getter.\n\n4. **`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 The create audit line now records `membershipLevel={}` (per the spec's instruction that anything recording the tier must record the level instead), and I removed a now-stale comment referencing the old GOLD tier.\n\n### Verification\nFull suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**. The acceptance test creates an owner with an email and a unique name (so `namesakeCount` is 0), expecting `membershipLevel == 3` (1 + email + namesake-0, capped at 3), which passes. The audit output confirms `membershipLevel=3`.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the codebase's create audit line did **not** previously record the tier, but the spec explicitly named it (\"such as the create audit line... must record the level\"), so I added `membershipLevel` to it. If you'd prefer the audit line left untouched, that's a one-line revert.",
